ta-line":"","children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#005CC5"},"children":"                yaml_tag_directive_t"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":" *"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#24292E"},"children":"end;"}]]}],"\n",["$","span",null,{"data-line":"","children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#24292E"},"children":"            } tag_directives;"}]}],"\n",["$","span",null,{"data-line":"","children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":"            int"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#24292E"},"children":" implicit;"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#6A737D"},"children":"                                   // int as bool"}]]}],"\n","$L52","\n","$L53","\n","$L54","\n","$L55","\n","$L56","\n","$L57","\n","$L58","\n","$L59","\n","$L5a","\n","$L5b","\n","$L5c","\n","$L5d","\n","$L5e","\n","$L5f","\n","$L60","\n","$L61","\n","$L62","\n","$L63","\n","$L64","\n","$L65"]}]}]}],"$L66","$L67","$L68","$L69","$L6a","$L6b","$L6c","$L6d","$L6e","$L6f","$L70"]}]]}]}]
19:["$","p",null,{"children":"The central challenge in defining equivalence is specification ambiguity: expressing what the code should do when moving to a fundamentally different language paradigm. We take a test-driven development approach, where the specification is expressed using test suites."}]
1a:["$","p",null,{"children":"The challenges in this approach manifest in several ways:"}]
1b:["$","ul",null,{"children":["\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Incomplete specifications:"}]," tests tell you what the code should do or what features should be included, but often tests do not cover the full intended functionalities of the target program."]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Implementation versus intent:"}]," Even when there is full test coverage, not all functionality makes sense in Rust, such as tests on pointer arithmetic in C."]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Specification errors:"}]," Test suites can be flawed and have contradictions between individual test cases."]}],"\n",["$","li",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Scale:"}]," At production scale, test suites often require far more code than the programs they verify. This challenges agents to reason over a large number of possible test compositions and edge cases."]}],"\n"]}]
1c:["$","p",null,{"children":"These complexities in rigorous system design push the limits of AI agents and their architecture itself."}]
1d:["$","p",null,{"children":"Long-horizon inconsistency makes it difficult to maintain correctness across thousands of reasoning steps, where small early errors compound into major failures. Context window limits degrade the ability of AI agents to reason about large amounts of code and tests. Agents also need language-specific or application-specific development tools but AI models might not be trained to use these specialized tools."}]
1e:["$","h2",null,{"id":"human-ai-test-driven-development","children":"Human-AI test-driven development"}]
1f:["$","p",null,{"children":"Building production-ready software is a chicken-and-egg problem: writing comprehensive and correct tests requires understanding how the code will be written, but writing the code requires knowing how it will be tested."}]
20:["$","p",null,{"children":"To tackle these challenges, we developed agents centered on rigorous, iterative, and collaborative test-driven development (TDD). Our agents follow high-level goals and guardrails, while ensuring every specification and test case is met. These architectures use primitives and workflows that optimize for expressiveness, efficiency, and correctness across thousands of iterations and large test suites."}]
21:["$","div",null,{"style":{"width":"100%","margin":"0.5rem 0","textAlign":"center","display":"flex","justifyContent":"center"},"children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/asari-website-proposal/original-asari/assets/blog-visuals/agent_workflow.svg","alt":"Collaborative human-AI test-driven development workflow","className":"h-auto w-full md:max-w-[60%]"}]}]
22:["$","div",null,{"className":"text-content-secondary font-family-sans text-center","style":{"fontSize":"0.75rem","marginTop":"0.25rem"},"children":["$","p",null,{"children":"While humans provide high-level goals and resolve critical design decisions,\nour agent autonomously executes the translation through iterative cycles of\ndesign, implementation, verification, and optimization."}]}]
23:["$","p",null,{"children":"Our agents also effectively collaborate with humans to make key design decisions and test development, adapting existing tests and creating new ones optimized for performance, code coverage, and coding style. A key advantage is their ability to search over design decisions and identify gaps in specifications. For instance, adapting C's memory model to Rust's ownership system often requires rethinking data access and usage patterns, a form of invention that goes beyond mechanical translation."}]
24:["$","p",null,{"children":"Throughout the translation process, agents required very little human guidance, typically 2-10 critical decision points per library where human judgment shaped architectural choices or resolved specification ambiguities. This approach is also economically efficient: running costs ranged from $50-$500 per library, without explicitly optimizing the process for cost."}]
25:["$","h2",null,{"id":"verifying-software-for-space","children":"Verifying software for space"}]
26:["$","blockquote",null,{"className":"border-brand-tertiary text-brand-primary/80 bg-brand-tertiary/10 my-6 border-l-4 py-4 pl-6 text-lg font-medium italic","style":{"fontFamily":"'Didact Gothic', 'Inter', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif"},"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"“The additional help of AI in verification and validation processes can\ncontribute to an increase in reliability, understanding, and level of trust in\nthe software we use to fly.”"}],["$","footer",null,{"className":"text-brand-tertiary mt-2 text-sm font-medium not-italic","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"— GTD GmbH"}]}]]}]
27:["$","p",null,{"children":["Building rigorous, safe systems requires a detailed understanding of intent. To test our agents with expert feedback, we worked with ",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://gtd-gmbh.de/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"external-link-in-content","children":"GTD GmbH"}],", a trusted test provider that verifies flight software to the strict accuracy and reliability standards set by the ",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://www.esa.int/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"external-link-in-content","children":"European Space Agency"}],". GTD provided a comprehensive test suite for ",["$","code",null,{"children":"libmcs"}],", a high-precision arithmetic library that performs mission-critical calculations for satellite navigation, orbital mechanics, and flight control systems."]}]
28:["$","p",null,{"children":"When code runs in space, even seemingly simple operations like computing a square root require high precision, because the slightest numerical error can cascade into mission failure."}]
29:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 my-4","children":["$","details",null,{"className":"group","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"cursor-pointer hover:bg-slate-100 p-2 rounded transition-colors duration-200 font-bold text-lg list-none flex items-center gap-2","style":{"fontFamily":"var(--font-family-sans)"},"children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4 sm:h-4 sm:w-4 h-5 w-5 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-90","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","path","mthhwq",{"d":"m9 18 6-6-6-6"}],"$undefined"]}],["$","p",null,{"children":["Deep Dive: The software implementation of ",["$","code",null,{"children":"sqrt"}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-slate-700 text-sm","style":{"fontFamily":"var(--font-family-sans)","overflow":"visible"},"children":[["$","style",null,{"jsx":"true","children":"\ndiv pre {\n  overflow-x: auto !important;\n  white-space: pre !important;\n  word-break: normal !important;\n  -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;\n}\ndiv pre code {\n  white-space: pre !important;\n  word-break: normal !important;\n}\nh1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {\n  font-family: var(--font-family-sans) !important;\n  font-weight: bold !important;\n}\n"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["On most modern CPUs, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"sqrt"}]," is implemented with a dedicated hardware instruction (e.g., x86 has ",["$","code",null,{"children":"FSQRT"}]," on x87 and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"SQRTSD/SQRTSS"}]," on SSE/AVX). However, when hardware ",["$","code",null,{"children":"sqrt"}]," isn't available, or when a library wants predictable, correctly rounded results without relying on floating-point hardware, it uses a digit-recurrence algorithm."]}],["$","br",null,{}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Unlike the Newton-Raphson root-finding method, which approximates and refines,\nthis method acts like \"long division\" but for square roots. It determines the\nresult bit-by-exact-bit, ensuring the final answer is perfectly rounded\naccording to IEEE 754 standards."}],["$","br",null,{}],["$","p",null,{"children":["As with most ",["$","code",null,{"children":"libm"}],"-style implementations, special cases such as NaN, ±∞,\nnegative inputs, and ±0 are handled up front and return immediately. The\nremainder of the algorithm assumes a finite, non-negative, non-zero input and\nfocuses on computing the correctly rounded significand."]}],["$","hr",null,{}],["$","h4",null,{"children":"1. Split the float into raw bits"}],["$","figure",null,{"data-rehype-pretty-code-figure":"","children":["$","pre",null,{"style":{"backgroundColor":"#fff","color":"#24292e"},"tabIndex":"0","data-language":"rust","data-theme":"github-light","children":["$","code",null,{"children":[["$","span",null,{"data-line":"","children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":"let"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#24292E"},"children":" sign"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":":"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#6F42C1"},"children":" u32"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":" ="}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#005CC5"},"children":" 0x80000000"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#24292E"},"children":";"}]]}],"\n",["$","span",null,{"data-line":"","children":" "}],"\n",["$","span",null,{"data-line":"","children":["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#6A737D"},"children":"// Extract the bit representation"}]}],"\n",["$","span",null,{"data-line":"","children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":"let"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#24292E"},"children":" bits "}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":"="}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#24292E"},"children":" x"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":"."}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#6F42C1"},"children":"to_bits"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#24292E"},"children":"();"}]]}],"\n",["$","span",null,{"data-line":"","children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":"let"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"color":"#D73A49"},"children":" mut"}],"$L71","$L72","$L73","$L74","$L75","$L76","$L77","$L78","$L79"]}],"\n","$L7a"]}]}]}],"$L7b","$L7c","$L7d","$L7e","$L7f","$L80","$L81","$L82","$L83","$L84","$L85","$L86","$L87","$L88","$L89","$L8a","$L8b","$L8c","$L8d","$L8e","$L8f","$L90","$L91","$L92","$L93","$L94","$L95","$L96","$L97","$L98","$L99","$L9a","$L9b","$L9c","$L9d","$L9e","$L9f","$La0","$La1","$La2","$La3","$La4","$La5","$La6","$La7","$La8"]}]]}]}]
2a:["$","p",null,{"children":"Our agents went above and beyond the extreme standards by efficiently surfacing subtle inconsistencies in the tests and verification process for human review."}]
2b:["$","p",null,{"children":"As GTD's team notes:"}]
2c:["$","blockquote",null,{"children":["\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"“We have been very positively surprised by the fact that Asari AI has been able to come back to us with such in-depth comments and assessment about the obtained test results and slightest differences in numerical behavior.\" -GTD"}],"\n"]}]
2d:["$","p",null,{"children":"Our agents found several previously undetected errors in the verification process. GTD highlighted one particularly tricky case:"}]
2e:["$","blockquote",null,{"children":["\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"“Asari AI's agents were able to find a very subtle double error in our test suite which we never spotted throughout years of reviews.\""}],"\n"]}]
2f:["$","p",null,{"children":"This thoroughness in understanding tests and design choices proved essential for maintaining the rigorous correctness standards throughout the Rust translation."}]
30:["$","p",null,{"children":"The GTD team reflects on the broader significance:"}]
31:["$","blockquote",null,{"children":["\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"“Space software needs to be well understood, but requirements engineering, verification, and validation are open ended tasks. The additional help of AI in verification and validation processes can contribute to an increase in reliability, understanding, and level of trust in the software we use to fly.\""}],"\n"]}]
32:["$","h2",null,{"id":"safe-not-slow","children":"Safe, not slow"}]
33:["$","p",null,{"children":"When migrating software, retaining performance is often non-negotiable."}]
34:["$","p",null,{"children":"We measured performance of our Safe Rust programs across multiple dimensions: successful memory safe translation, equivalence testing, performance overhead, memory footprint changes, and tool runtime. In addition, we evaluated qualitative measures of code quality and whether the output is idiomatic Rust, not just syntactically correct code."}]
35:["$","p",null,{"children":"Besides being correct, our Safe Rust code has comparable performance as the original C code. We also believe that our agents can further optimize the Rust implementations to be even more performant than it is currently."}]
36:["$","h2",null,{"id":"safe-not-slop","children":"Safe, not slop"}]
37:["$","blockquote",null,{"className":"border-brand-tertiary text-brand-primary/80 bg-brand-tertiary/10 my-6 border-l-4 py-4 pl-6 text-lg font-medium italic","style":{"fontFamily":"'Didact Gothic', 'Inter', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif"},"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"\"I would probably be fooling myself if I could say that I could tell that this\ncode was machine translated from C.\""}],["$","footer",null,{"className":"text-brand-tertiary mt-2 text-sm font-medium not-italic","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"— Senior Rust Developer"}]}]]}]
38:["$","p",null,{"children":"Besides ensuring correctness and good performance, our agents are notably much less prone to producing “AI slop”. In many cases, the Safe Rust code written by our agents reads like it was written by a skilled human engineer."}]
39:["$","p",null,{"children":["An external senior Rust developer noted that the Rust implementation of ",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://github.com/Asari-AI/gzip-safe-rust/tree/main/gzip_redesign","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"external-link-in-content","children":["$","code",null,{"children":"gzip"}]}]," felt like the work of a competent engineer:"]}]
3a:["$","blockquote",null,{"children":["\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"“It feels like work done by a Rust engineer with 3-5 years of experience. The code is actually well structured and components are well encapsulated.”"}],"\n"]}]
3b:["$","p",null,{"children":"Importantly, our agents’ code lacks the tell-tale signs of machine generation:"}]
3c:["$","blockquote",null,{"children":["\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"“I would probably be fooling myself if I could say that I could tell that this code was machine translated from C.”"}],"\n"]}]
3d:["$","p",null,{"children":"This idiomatic quality, not just syntactic correctness, is essential for maintainability and long-term adoption."}]
3e:["$","h2",null,{"id":"from-faithful-translations-to-full-redesigns","children":"From faithful translations to full redesigns"}]
3f:["$","p",null,{"children":"One important decision is the degree of redesign. At two ends of the spectrum, either 1) produce faithful conversions that preserve C's structure, or 2) completely redesign the codebase and embrace Rust idioms. The degree of specification ambiguity shapes this choice; when specifications are less comprehensive, the agents have more freedom in their process. Our agents are flexible and can take either approach. In any case, the final result passes the same verification test suite."}]
40:["$","p",null,{"children":["The nature of these tradeoffs depends on the use case. For ",["$","code",null,{"children":"gzip"}],", the specification is relatively straightforward: ",["$","code",null,{"children":"gzip"}]," is a command-line executable with well-defined input/output behavior that all implementations should adhere to. This makes the tradeoff between faithful translation and idiomatic redesign more explicit, since validation tests can be defined independent of how the internal code is structured."]}]
